    {
      "id": "6d2bb2e3-2629-4f03-b0f1-56c0bdab1eef",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "### Goals for this project\n* Practice generating random numbers in specified ranges\n* Take advantage of randomness to generate arrays of students with random values for their attributes\n* Work with loops to examine the attributes of these students\n* Use these tools to evaluate a large number of students\n\n**Note**: You must have completed Project 4 before starting this project. You will be using ideas (and even code) from that project here.",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "6d2bb2e3-2629-4f03-b0f1-56c0bdab1eef"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "00edb2f7-7bde-413e-8b12-c313d3e824f9",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "#### Initial random numbers task\n\nJava provides a functionality called `Random` that lets us generate random numbers and random `boolean` (`true`/`false`) values. The next code cell gives three examples of generating random values. Run the cell several times to see that the values do change randomly.\n",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "00edb2f7-7bde-413e-8b12-c313d3e824f9"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "333b5233-93c0-46dc-9c0c-0ca548380a46",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "// Magic code: Build an object that can generate random numbers\n// Do not change this line of code\n\nRandom rand = new Random();\n\n// Example 1: Generate and display a random integer in the range [0, 99]\nint randomInteger = rand.nextInt(0, 100);\nSystem.out.println(\"Random integer in [0, 99]:\" + randomInteger);\n\n// Example 2: Generate and display a random decimal value in the range [1, 3.5]\ndouble randomDouble = rand.nextDouble(1, 3.5);\nSystem.out.println(\"Random decimal in [1, 3.5]:\" + randomDouble);\n\n// Example 3: Generate and display a random true/false value\nboolean randomBoolean = rand.nextBoolean();\nSystem.out.println(\"Random boolean: \" + randomBoolean);\n\n",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "46647b3c-a8b0-42a0-9b2f-09addea0ea94",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Using those examples, complete this task by adding code to the next cell that meets the following goals.\n\n* Generate and display a random integer in the range [10, 500]\n* Generate and display a random decimal value in the range [-5.5, 7.5]\n* Generate and display a random decimal value in the range [0, 2 $\\pi$] (Remember that you can use `Math.PI` to get the value of $\\pi$ that Java thinks is most precise.)",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "46647b3c-a8b0-42a0-9b2f-09addea0ea94"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "5c3da54c-259d-4664-9bce-e383c20f825d",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "// Add code for these goals below this line.",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "11d04d21-c155-44f6-9bbc-ac9a834a7586",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "#### Extending random numbers task\n\nSometimes we want to generate a random integer from an evenly spaced set of integers. For example, suppose we want to select a value from the set [3, 8, 13, 18, 23, 28]. Here is the logic we use.\n\n* First, there are six values in the set. So we start with \n\n```java\nrand.nextInt(0, 7)\n```\n\nto generate a number from the range [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5].\n\n* Then, the integers we want have a spacing of 5. If we multiply the random number by 5:\n\n```java\n5 * rand.nextInt(0, 6)\n```\n\nour range is now [0, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25].\n\n* Finally, the first value in our set is 3, so we add 3 to each value:\n```java\n5 * rand.nextInt(0, 6) + 3\n```\n\nto get [3, 8, 13, 18, 23, 28]. \n\nThe next code cell has this code so that you can test that the random numbers this expression generates are only within that set. Run the cell a few times to verify that.\n\nComplete this task by generating a random integer in the range [-8, -6, -4, -2, 0, 2, 4, 6, 8].\n",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "11d04d21-c155-44f6-9bbc-ac9a834a7586"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c11862fe-3aa3-4476-85d6-dd7def7f3568",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "// Code for the example\n\nint spacedInteger = 5 * rand.nextInt(0, 6) + 3; \nSystem.out.println(\"Spaced integer: \" + spacedInteger);\n\n// Add code below this line to complete the task\n",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "871b6b6d-fe86-4d43-9156-a83f51df1c44",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "*Note:* The following cell contains code to calculate registration points like in the last project. Run the cell now, but don't make any changes to it.",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "871b6b6d-fe86-4d43-9156-a83f51df1c44"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "8ef76c3a-28f5-4524-a1f1-3442d329ddd0",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "// Magic code: This cell contains a basic version of calcTotalCredits() and calculatePoints()\n// Do not modify this code\n\npublic double calcTotalCredits(double guilfordCredits, double transferCredits, double otherCredits) {\n    return guilfordCredits + transferCredits + otherCredits;\n}\n\npublic double calculatePoints(double gCredits, double tCredits, \n    double oCredits, boolean studyAway) {\n\n    double registrationPoints = 0;\n    double totalCredits = calcTotalCredits(gCredits, tCredits, oCredits);\n    if (totalCredits >= 88) {\n        registrationPoints += 4;\n    }\n\n    if (totalCredits >= 56 && totalCredits <= 87) {\n        registrationPoints += 3;\n    }\n\n    if (totalCredits >= 24 && totalCredits <= 56) {\n        registrationPoints += 2;\n    }\n\n    if (totalCredits < 24) {\n        registrationPoints += 1;\n    }\n\n    if (studyAway) {\n        registrationPoints *= 1.25;\n    }\n\n    if (tCredits > gCredits || oCredits > gCredits || tCredits + oCredits > gCredits) {\n        registrationPoints /= 2;\n    }\n\n    if (totalCredits > 120) {\n        registrationPoints *= 1.5;\n    }\n    \n\n    return registrationPoints;\n}",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java"
      },
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "id": "f6c24f4f-a58e-4e33-b686-8687d4dc8876",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "#### Attribute range task\n\nIn the previous project, you started the process of defining attributes for a Guilford student that would help determine the priority they would have for registration. Here are the four attributes that were originally defined with the identifier we used. All are decimal values, except for the last one that can be `true` or `false`.\n\n* Credits earned at Guilford: `guilfordCredits` \n* Transfer credits: `transferCredits`\n* Other credits: `otherCredits`\n* Is the student studying away?: `studyAway`.\n\nIn the next code cell, write code that declares each of these variables and assigns a random value to it in the following ranges.\n\n* `guilfordCredits`: [0, 92]\n* `transferCredits`: [0, 32]\n* `otherCredits`: [0, 12]\n* `studyAway`: `true` or `false`\n\nWrite statements that display each of these values as we've done before. Run the cell a few times to convince yourself that the randomness is present.\n\nAfter you do that, run the cell that follows to define a simple `calculatePoints` method. If you've done everything correctly, you should see a registration points value. ",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "f6c24f4f-a58e-4e33-b686-8687d4dc8876"
      }
    },
