      "id": "a29395ad-190d-448b-b7dd-fc4b6cc4f936",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "#### Summarizing data using histograms task\n\nIn analyzing whether an algorithm is working effectively to evaluate information about an individual, we often want to have summary information about all the students. One type of summary is a ***histogram***, which counts the data into equally spaced bins. Let's assume that the values will fall between 0 and 10 points and we'll use 10 bins. \n\n(**Note** that you don't write any code here; this section just provides the overall logic you should understand.)\n\n* Declare and instantiate an array with ten elements that represent the ten bins with subranges 0-1, 1-2, 2-3, and so on, up to > 9 points. \n* Loop through the students.\n* For each student, get their score and find which subrange it belongs to. (We'll use rounding.)\n* Increment the corresponding bin by one.\n* When we're done with the loop, display the result using `Arrays.toString(histogram)`.\n\n\nYou'll now implement this plan in the next code cell to create a histogram of the `registrationPoints` array. The only part that is new is the subrange part. For our data, if you use the identifier `histogram` for the histogram array and `index` for the counter, then the following statements will work:\n\n```java\nint bin = (int)registrationPoints[index];\nhistogram[bin]++;\n```\n\nAdd statements to the next code cell that implement this plan.\n",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "a29395ad-190d-448b-b7dd-fc4b6cc4f936"
      }
    },
