    {
      "id": "b3ae3556-8229-4ac4-a9b2-eb9c2a41e3c6",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "#### Optional challenge exercises\n\nHere are several optional challenge exercises.\n\n* Our use of `nextBoolean` in setting the value for whether the student is studying away gives that a 50% chance, which is far too high. Implement instead a conditional statement in the appropriate loop above that changes that percentage. The basic logic is to generate a random decimal value between 0 and 1. Then, if that value is less than whatever cutoff you decide on (like 0.05 for a 5% change), set the study away value to `true`. Otherwise set it to false.\n* All our registration point values turn out to be between 0 and 10, so it's easy to set the index value when we have 10 bins. Try rewriting the code so that it works for any number of bins. Assume that the range of each bin is equally spaced. \n    * How will you calculate the bin that each value goes into?\n    * What would you do if that calculation gives a negative value or a value larger than the largest bin index?\n* Related to the previous challenge exercise, prompt the user for the number of students and the number of bins to use in the histogram.\n* What we've done so far relies on our knowing that the data we're analyzing falls in the range \\[0, 10\\]. But what if it doesn't? Write a method that can create a histogram with 10 bins out of any numeric data without initial knowledge of the range. Doing so requires at least the following:\n    * Finding the minimum and maximum value of the elements in the array. You can write a loop to accomplish this goal or research Java utilities that can help, such as `Collections.min()` and `Collections.max()`.\n    * Given this information and the number of bins, calculating the size of each bin (that is, what part of the total range it covers).\n    * For a given value, determining which bin it falls into.\n    * And, of course, you can generalize your work to any number of bins.\n* Exercise 7.7 in the Downey and Mayfield textbook. Note that the return type is an array. And you can test this method with very long strings. \n",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "b3ae3556-8229-4ac4-a9b2-eb9c2a41e3c6"
      }
    }
